# Paths reqmap should not scan (one fnmatch glob per line, # comments ok).

# The vendored engine and its viewer template. reqmap.py carries the `implements:`
# self-tags of the repository it was written in, and those requirements do not live
# here — unignored, every one reads as a dangling ERROR. This is the standard consumer
# posture the engine seeds itself (`_reqmapignore_seed`); this repo is a consumer of
# reqmap, not its home.
scripts/reqmap.py
scripts/_map_viewer.html

# Isolated agent worktrees. Each holds a FULL second copy of this repo, so a local scan
# counts every member twice and reads the copies' tags as dangling refs — errors that do
# not exist in the code, in files CI never checks out.
.worktrees/**
.claude/worktrees/**

# Generated diagrams: regenerable by definition, never committed.
out/**
diagrams/**
__pycache__/**

# Worked example generators. They demonstrate the builder rather than implementing
# a capability of their own, so tagging one would claim a requirement it does not
# carry. They are not unchecked: CI runs every generator, and a generator that
# raises fails the build.
plugin/skills/excalidraw-diagram/examples/**

# The diagram generators behind README.md's pictures. Same argument as the worked
# examples above: a generator demonstrates the builder rather than implementing a
# capability, so drafting a requirement for one claims a promise it does not carry.
# CI runs each of them, so they are checked, just not owned by a requirement.
docs/make_*.py

# The contract and its reference files. SKILL.md is already in the engine's ignore
# bucket by name; these are the same contract (SKILL.universal.md) and the material
# that illustrates it. What they describe is owned by ARCH-EXCALIDRAW-030/031/034 —
# drafting a second requirement from the prose would state the same obligation twice,
# which is the merge smell the engine warns about, seeded on purpose.
plugin/skills/excalidraw-diagram/SKILL.universal.md
plugin/skills/excalidraw-diagram/references/**
